  "body": "## Finding description and impact\n\n**Description**\n\nAccording to the project team, it is intended behavior that an expired order can be \"revived\" by amending it with a new, future `cancelTimestamp`. This functionality works as expected when the order's `price` or `side` is changed, as this flow is handled by `_executeAmendNewOrder`, which re-creates the order with all new parameters, including the new `cancelTimestamp`.\n\nHowever, if a user attempts to revive an expired order by only changing its `amountInBase` (and providing a new `cancelTimestamp`), the amendment is processed by `_executeAmendAmount`. This function only updates the order's `amount` and does not update the `cancelTimestamp`.\n\n```solidity\n// contracts/clob/CLOB.sol: _executeAmendAmount\nfunction _executeAmendAmount(Book storage ds, Order storage order, uint256 amount)\n    internal\n    returns (int256 quoteTokenDelta, int256 baseTokenDelta)\n{\n    if (order.side == Side.BUY) {\n        int256 oldAmountInQuote = ds.getQuoteTokenAmount(order.price, order.amount).toInt256();\n        int256 newAmountInQuote = ds.getQuoteTokenAmount(order.price, amount).toInt256();\n\n        quoteTokenDelta = oldAmountInQuote - newAmountInQuote;\n\n        ds.metadata().quoteTokenOpenInterest =\n            uint256(ds.metadata().quoteTokenOpenInterest.toInt256() - quoteTokenDelta);\n    } else {\n        baseTokenDelta = order.amount.toInt256() - amount.toInt256();\n\n        ds.metadata().baseTokenOpenInterest =\n            uint256(ds.metadata().baseTokenOpenInterest.toInt256() - baseTokenDelta);\n    }\n\n    order.amount = amount;\n    // Missing: order.cancelTimestamp is not updated here.\n}\n```\n\nAs a result, the expired order remains expired on the books. While it is not immediately canceled, it is effectively unusable and will be automatically removed and refunded the next time it is encountered during the matching process, failing to achieve the user's and the protocol's intended outcome of reviving the order.\n\n**Impact**\n\nThis is a functional bug that leads to inconsistent behavior and breaks a core design intention of the protocol. Users who try to revive an expired order by adjusting its size will find their transaction succeeds, but the order remains expired and non-functional. \n\n---\n\n## Recommended mitigation steps\n\nTo ensure consistent behavior and align with the protocol's intent, the `cancelTimestamp` should be updated in all amendment scenarios.\n\nIn the `_processAmend` function, before branching into `_executeAmendNewOrder` or `_executeAmendAmount`, unconditionally update the `order.cancelTimestamp`.\n\nAlternatively, add `order.cancelTimestamp = uint32(args.cancelTimestamp);` inside the `_executeAmendAmount` function. \n\n---",
  "proof_of_concept": "```solidity\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity ^0.8.0;\n\nimport {PoCTestBase} from \"./PoCTestBase.t.sol\";\nimport {Side, Order} from \"contracts/clob/types/Order.sol\";\nimport {ICLOB} from \"contracts/clob/ICLOB.sol\";\nimport {CLOB} from \"contracts/clob/CLOB.sol\";\nimport {console} from \"forge-std/Test.sol\";\n\ncontract PoC is PoCTestBase {\n    /**\n     * @notice PoC for Vulnerability: Amending only the amount of an order fails to update its `cancelTimestamp`.\n     * @dev This PoC demonstrates that when a user calls `amend` to change only the `amountInBase` of an existing order,\n     *      the `cancelTimestamp` provided in the arguments is ignored. The order retains its original, older timestamp,\n     *      which can lead to premature expiration against the user's intent.\n     */\n    function test_submissionValidity() external {\n        _poc_amendAmountIgnoresTimestamp();\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * @dev PoC for the timestamp update vulnerability in amount-only amendments.\n     */\n    function _poc_amendAmountIgnoresTimestamp() internal {\n        console.log(\"\\n--- Running PoC: Amending order amount ignores new cancelTimestamp ---\");\n        \n        // --- 1. Setup ---\n        \n        // Prepare roles and order parameters.\n        address alice = users[0]; // The user who will place and amend the order.\n        address bob = users[1];   // The user who will trigger the expired order cleanup.\n        address carol = users[2]; // Provides a valid order to ensure Bob's transaction succeeds.\n        \n        uint256 price = 100 ether;\n        uint256 initialAmount = 2 ether;\n        uint32 initialExpiry = uint32(block.timestamp + 1 hours);\n\n        // Prepare funds and place sell orders for Alice and Carol.\n        // Alice's order is set to expire in 1 hour.\n        setupOrder(Side.SELL, alice, initialAmount, price, initialExpiry);\n        // Carol's order never expires and will serve as the counterparty for Bob.\n        setupOrder(Side.SELL, carol, 1 ether, price, 0);\n\n        // Verify the timestamp of Alice's initial order.\n        Order memory orderBefore = clob.getOrder(1); // Alice's order ID is 1.\n        assertEq(orderBefore.cancelTimestamp, initialExpiry);\n        console.log(\"Log: Alice placed an order with a 1-hour expiry.\");\n\n        // --- 2. Execution ---\n\n        // Alice amends her order, reducing the amount and attempting to extend the expiry to 24 hours.\n        vm.prank(alice);\n        clob.amend(alice, ICLOB.AmendArgs({\n            orderId: 1, // Alice's order ID.\n            amountInBase: 1 ether, // New amount.\n            price: price,\n            side: Side.SELL,\n            cancelTimestamp: uint32(block.timestamp + 24 hours), // New, longer expiry time.\n            limitOrderType: ICLOB.LimitOrderType.POST_ONLY\n        }));\n        console.log(\"Log: Alice amended her order, attempting to extend its expiry to 24 hours.\");\n\n        // --- 3. Verification ---\n\n        // Check the order's timestamp after the amendment to prove it was not updated.\n        Order memory orderAfter = clob.getOrder(1);\n        assertEq(orderAfter.cancelTimestamp, initialExpiry, \"FAIL: The order's cancelTimestamp was not updated.\");\n        console.log(\"Log: Verified that the timestamp was not updated and remains the original 1-hour expiry.\");\n        \n        // --- 4. Consequence Demonstration ---\n        \n        // Warp time to a point after the original expiry.\n        vm.warp(initialExpiry + 1 minutes);\n        console.log(\"Log: Time warped past the original expiry.\");\n\n        // Bob initiates a Taker buy order to trigger the cleanup mechanism. His amount is sufficient to fill Carol's order.\n        uint256 bobAmount = 1 ether;\n        setupTokens(Side.BUY, bob, bobAmount, price, true);\n        vm.prank(bob);\n        clob.postFillOrder(bob, ICLOB.PostFillOrderArgs({\n            amount: bobAmount,\n            priceLimit: price,\n            side: Side.BUY,\n            amountIsBase: true,\n            fillOrderType: ICLOB.FillOrderType.IMMEDIATE_OR_CANCEL\n        }));\n        console.log(\"Log: A trade was initiated by Bob, triggering cleanup of expired orders.\");\n\n        // Final verification: Alice's order should now be removed from the book because it expired.\n        Order memory finalOrderState = clob.getOrder(1);\n        assertEq(finalOrderState.owner, address(0), \"FAIL: The order should have been removed, but it still exists.\");\n        \n        console.log(\"Log: SUCCESS! PoC confirmed that the ignored timestamp led to the order's premature removal.\");\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * @dev Helper function to reduce code duplication in setup.\n     *      It prepares funds and places a limit order in one step.\n     */\n    function setupOrder(Side side, address user, uint256 amount, uint256 price, uint32 expiry) internal {\n        setupTokens(side, user, amount, price, true);\n        vm.prank(user);\n        clob.postLimitOrder(user, ICLOB.PostLimitOrderArgs({\n            amountInBase: amount,\n            price: price,\n            cancelTimestamp: expiry,\n            side: side,\n            clientOrderId: 0,\n            limitOrderType: ICLOB.LimitOrderType.GOOD_TILL_CANCELLED\n        }));\n    }\n}\n```",
